 gene expression which helps to  quantify noise and here we're looking at  the full change between  human gene expression at five nanograms  and 100 nanogram library input  the expectation is that uh the signal  should line up across the zero line if  there is no fold change between the two  different inputs and as you can see here  that  expectation is met showing total input  rna has little to no effect on noise  so as i mentioned in the previous slides  the platform slides for the technology  and our full solution  you know i covered the percentage and  rate of depletion across different  organisms as well as the stability of  ercc spike ins at different inputs and  finally the  consistency of human gene expression  across different inputs as well  now that i've shown that data i want to  move on to show the in vitro use of the  crispr cast system and how it improves  detection sensitivity of metagenomics in  gs  before i show any data i first want to  show the study design we employed  we used coveted samples for the study  design of course the coveted genome is  serving as a proxy for any infectious  disease here but coveted samples are  relatively easy to get a hold of right  now  we use 60 nasopharyngeal samples 45  covet positive  ranging in cycle threshold values from  rt pcr from 16 to 39  and we had 15 covet negative samples  each one of these was split into three  where one consisting of one control and  two replicas for depletion each one of  these then went through sequencing  library construction and depletion  so we had 180 samples  there  moved into sequencing sequencing at 2 by  150 bases  and then down sampling the data to 80  million reads or 40 million clusters  we then employed a five-prong analysis  approach using idc for pathogen  detection sars genome assembly niad  priority pathogen assessment and g said  clade identif identity uh for the  genomes  we star for alignments and gatk for  variant calling  kraken 2  we used as a k-mer-based  system for interrogating the composition  of the samples  a pipeline called dgnr for differential  expression for host response and then  also custom in-house pipelines for  computing depletion rates  where we use the silva database which is  a compendium of all known ribosomal rna  sequences  i'm going to show data from each of  these analysis today  i'm showing the performance of the chris  crisper clean plus system for depletion  of ribosomal rna  we've taken a subset of the samples from  the 60 sample set and we're showing here  a covet negative on the far left moving  up through  the cycle threshold values from 16 to 38  showing a total of seven samples here  you can see that  the nasopharyngeal samples the  the percentage of mapped reads to  ribosomal rna which is shown on the  y-axis here  the percentage map breach varies from  sample to sample but we are very  effectively able to reduce the ribosomal  content from the human  from human ribosomal rna and from  bacterial ribosomal rna  in these samples  so what we're showing in this slide is  our ability to call a cova genome strain  as well as detect the cova genome using  idc and then next strain for the strain  calling  you can see that the samples here that  are in green in the table  these are samples that we have been able  to call the strain and and what we mean  by that is are we calling the strain of  of the cova genome through neck strain  uh far down the clad tree and do the  replicates uh for those samples both or  for the replicates have the same strain  call  showing consistency there and you can  see down to approximately a cycle  threshold of 30  we are getting a confidence strain call  for these samples  next when we think about simply  detecting the covet genome not  necessarily calling the stream but  detecting it and thinking about  detection of infectious diseases in the  future things that we may not know what  the genome sequence is and we're unable  to call a strain but we know enough of  the genome that we can detect it you can  see here that we're able to push the  level of detection down  or push it up to a cycle threshold  around 36. how we define that as  detected and non-strain called is that  each of the replicates may not share the  same strain call or  there's been enough genome sequence for  for idc to assemble contigs of the  genome but there's not enough for next  strain to confidently call that strain  and thus it places it simply on the  trunk of the clayed tree which is the  19a essentially  so here you can see we're able to  with  simple metagen simple metagenomic ngs  shotgun sequencing and the crispr clean  plus  application to drive our cova detection  all the way down to a ct of 36. this is  a very exciting results  as i mentioned earlier this data is also  down sampled to 80 million reads or 40  million clusters and and so  this is a modest amount of sequencing  when thinking about today's cost of  sequencing  next using in-house and custom pipelines  we can look at things like what's the  breadth of coveted  genome coverage or breadth of assembly  that we could derive from our sequencing  data down sample to 80 million reads and  what's the depth of genome coverage and  how is that affected by cycle threshold  so you can see on the left here that  we're seeing 100 genome coverage of the  cova genome um up to about a cycle  threshold of 27 and then we see that  decrease  as we move beyond 27 up to 32 and then  going down below 10 genome coverage as  we move into cycle thresholds of 35 and  higher  of even more interest is our depth of  coverage and and why i mention that is  because of the linearity that we see  of the depth of coverage here uh versus  cycle threshold and thus we in some ways  we have the ability to  pin our average genome coverage uh to a  cycle threshold uh and this is this is  interesting uh for in a number of ways  but um here also just to mention i'm  showing an average of the replicates  here and um  and you can see that even up to um up to  a level of say  33 or 32  cts we're still obtaining greater than  one x depth of coverage across the cova  genome  in the next four slides i'm going to  spend some time showing some data around  composition  this is using our kraken pipeline  as well as some information from idc  as i mentioned earlier  the coveted genome in the study  can be thought of as a proxy as well for  other infectious diseases things that  could be emerging  or things that are already known about  but  understanding what  additional information we can gain  around composition using the crispr  clean plus approach i think is is high  value  and here again we're showing the seven  samples that we've been zooming in on  and ultimately as you can see from the  breadth and depth of coverage graphs i  showed you earlier really representative  of the whole set of samples  but  when we look at something like a mock  sample which essentially has no  depletion  and that goes through a metagenomics  shotgun sequencing approach you can see  here that the bacterial species  identified or anywhere around 2500 to  3500 bacterial species but when we then  apply the depletion approach we can see  that now that we're removing those  ribosomal reeds that were previously  there  because we remove those ribosomal reeds  before they ever make it on a sequencer  we're now then opening up sequencing  real estate to reads that were  previously unsequenced in the mock  samples and now we're driving additional  coverage not only over bacterial species  that we already have seen but also  bacterial species that before were  hidden in the noise and now we're  bringing up  anywhere between you know another  thousand or five hundred fold bacterial  species in this sample this  increases the sensitivity of discovery  and it also increases your ability to  discover  new things about these samples that you  may not have known before things that  could serve as biomarkers or things that  can serve as potential um identification  for co-infections or for other types of  infections  that uh that may not be related to what  this person is actually presenting for  and this slide is showing exactly one of  those samples where we have a a covered  negative patient or sorry a code  somebody that's covered negative and  they're going in to have a test done  because they believe they may have covid  and  we can see from this from the shotgun  sequencing data that this is a  rhinovirus a in this negative sample  it's the highest scoring component in  both replicates from idc  and rhinovirus a is the it's one of the  most common  viral infectious agents in humans and  it's the predominant cause of common  cold so  it could be assumed from this that this  is one of one of the reasons uh that  this person went in to get a cova test  but we were able to detect this and if  um  you know under the state of doing an rt  pcr or even using a a primer set  directed towards covid this is something  that you would not pick up in the sample  and again here's another example we see  that um a co-infection in a  cova-positive sample so this is sample  403  the ct value of this sample was 26.53  showed up as a strong positive  but again in here rhinovirus c  co-infection in both replicates with an  extremely high score from idc and again  cause of common cold this is a  co-infection you can see again  a good breadth and depth of coverage  over the rhinovirus genome here and so  here now we're detecting co-infections  in a cova positive sample  and here we're showing an example that's  a little bit more exploratory  but this sample showed up with a high  score for  this  this virus called  babesiosis  it is a virus that's related to malaria  and um it  has a one to four week incubation period  but the symptoms include fever  general  general feeling of ill health fatigue  and loss of appetite again you can see  down below here in both of the  replicates that we have we have  significant genome coverage shown and uh  and albeit this may not be the reason  that they presented for covet test but  at the same time um this uh shows that  from an exploratory standpoint we're  able to increase discovery in these  types of samples  using the crispr clean plus  approach  so i'm going to spend the next couple  slides talking about host response we  undertook an analysis or transcriptional  analysis of using the human gene content  in these samples something that you  could not do unless you're doing a  shotgun metagenomics approach  but i'm going to talk about  some of the findings from this but  really we wanted to center on our  analysis on can we find pathways that  may be involved in um in the host  response to these infections and so in  that way we were able to analyze covet  negative the 15 cova negative versus a  subset of the of of covet positive  samples  first i wanted to take just a second to  show you the large amount of information  that's been generated around host  response  with regard to covet infection you can  see here these are papers  recent papers you know around the immune  and metabolic signatures of coven 19 and  immune signatures linked to severity  diagnostic host response biosignature  from rna profiling of nasal swabs and  host viral infection maps with  signatures for severity of covet 19.  you know this type of interrogation of  the host response with regards to an  infection  uh obviously has been extremely  important with with regards to kova 19.  but also  it will  continue to be extremely important as we  move forward and we have new strains  come online of influenza or even when we  think about  you know with this data with regards to  the next pandemic  to be able to  take a look at host response and  understand these signatures for the next  pandemic and do it as quickly as  possible  is going to take a shotgun metagenomics  approach and also it's going to take a  methodology  that removes the noise and  makes the signals more clear  and so what we've done here is we took  the 15 coveted negative samples  even though they may have presented with  a cold or other things like that we  still take the covet negative samples  and then we took  the covent positive samples that were  less than a ct of 22 which were 29  samples from those were able to generate  or sorry perform a differential  expression analysis  using degener  from that  we  found a uh signature of a hundred and  around 150 genes uh and then we're able  to look at those genes within  gene ontology pathways and you can see  here on the left the up regulated terms  in the covid positive samples  strongly related to immune system  so we've got t cell chemotaxis  regulation of natural killer cells  macrophage migration macrophage  chemotaxis lymph lymphocyte chemotaxis  this one has a  very very very significant p-value  eosinophil migration eosinophil  chemotaxis all of these movements of  immune cells  migrations and things like that  so we're easily able to identify  with statistical significance  these up regulated  pathways  in the  covid positive samples versus the  coveted negative samples by the way we  did this with no underlying metadata we  did not have any metadata concerning the  severity of the patient's response to  the covid uh in in the positive samples  the severity of the response and we  didn't have any metadata around the  negative samples and in fact as we know  from looking at one of the samples that  has rhinovirus in it that these were not  true negatives that were taking taken  from people that had no symptoms at all  whatsoever yet  still with uh with the lack of meta  information we're still able to find  these these statistically significant  up regulated immune pathways in the  covid positive samples  and so hopefully thus far  the uh presentation has has spurred on  some thoughts about how you might be  able to use um  the jump codes uh solution for your own  research or for other questions and and  certainly we think that way as well in r  d um sometimes good and sometimes bad  but um i'm going to show a couple slides  around other applications we've applied  this to and and these may be things that  you've you've thought about or were  potentially going to ask questions about  so hopefully i can answer some of those  now  so in the crispr clean plus approach we  were using guides designed  against the human  ribosomal non-coding ribosomal rna as  well as the bacterial ribosomal rna and  we want to be depleting those  transcripts but in the case of single  cell and isoseq  because those approaches are poly a  based at least right now  we want to be thinking about the  reduction of of other gene types since  the contribution of of non-coding  ribosomal is very small  and in this case we're really focusing  our attention at least  for some of this initial data on the  mitochondrial coding and ribosomal  coding transcripts and and so for  our single cell set initially we have  what we call our 9010 set you takes the  top 10  coding mitochondrial genes in the top 90  coding ribosomal genes and then we have  guides designed towards those for  depletion  and here we're showing the application  of that 9010 ribomido guide set to uh  coronary artery a single cell sequencing  and pulmonary artery single cell  sequencing on the 10x chromium platform  across multiple patients you can see  here that when  employing these guides for depletion in  these sample types within the 10x  library workflow  that we're we're generating extremely  high rated depletion we're destroying  greater than 90 percent of those  transcripts in there thus opening up  more space um for sequencing of  informative transcripts and uh for the  potential of identifying additional cell  types  we've also applied these guide sets and  methodology to the 10x vision system you  can see here on the left side the tissue  slices or the imaging of the tissue  slices where we have the depleted on the  far left and the control next to that  you can see that as we look down through  the the two mitochondrial genes and the  two  ribosomal genes  that are lighting up in the control  where we're greatly removing those into  the depleted sample  on the right side here and the violin  plots is simply showing the same thing  except in more of a a quantitative way  where you can see that we're  in the in the in the crisper samples uh  the signals from those those transcripts  are completely going away the number of  reads are are completely going away in  those as well  and so you know the depletion of these  targeted regions is significant and the  depletion across samples is significant  and as i mentioned before when we're  removing these transcripts prior to them  going on a sequencer  we then open up that area on the  sequencer to reassign reads that would  have originally gone to these  transcripts or gone to these genes we're  now reassigning those reads to other  genes  that are that are going to be more  informative  and so here we've also taken and applied  this same methodology to um  the uh  iso seek on the pacbio platform using a  10x chromium library system  and  you can see here as we look across these  single cell isoseq samples where we have  sample  a and sample b and we have rpl and rps  which are ribosomal targets  for each of those and then mt which is  our mitochondrial targets for each of  those  that we're uh those originally made up  around 15 percent of all of the data in  these samples and then once we depleted  uh those went down uh to nearly zero um  so freeing up 15 of that data on a pac  bio system for informative reads and you  can see here that  that the depletion has little effect on  on anything library related so the read  link differences between the bulk and  the single cell isoseq actually match  with the library sizes uh pre-sequencing  so there's there's there's no effect  that we're seeing there of the method  i think o