h you know identify and  assemble uh  the pathogen genome uh within 48 hours  great thank you  next question  does depletion increase read counts  across other things in the sample in an  even or uneven way  uh in an even way  typically that has to do with the  library construction process itself if  you have a good uh complex library  you've generated  minimizing pcr amplification  you typically see about uh a tenfold  increase at least with um  you know the 13 000 guide sets that were  used in the uh meta transcriptomic study  presented here  um knocking those out uh which is  roughly 90 percent of the reads in the  sample  you get on average a 10-fold increase  across all  non-targeted  species  thank you  it looks like we have time for one more  question  does sequencing to greater depth provide  higher sensitivity for variant calling  or covet detection  um so far what we've seen is that it  does not  as john presented here  each sample was down sampled to 40  million clusters or 80 million 2 by 150  paradigm reads  some of the samples were sequenced to  well over  50 60 million clusters or over 100 120  million reads and by adding those  additional reads uh back in  uh what we see is you get a increase in  depth of the same locations  of the cova genome not necessarily an  increase in breath  and what we're looking to determine  right now is  how much additional depletion  gives you an increase in sensitivity not  only to detect but to  cover more of the cover genome and we  hope to publish that data soon  great  well thank you again john and keith for  your time today in your important  research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor jump jumpcode genomics  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contac